<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eumenes of Cardia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 362–316 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eumenes of Cardia was a Greek general and scholar from Cardia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Thracian Chersonese</a> who served as royal secretary to Philip II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. After Alexander's death in 323 BCE he received Cappadocia and Paphlagonia and fought for the Argead royal house in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-diadochi">Wars of the Diadochi</a>. Betrayed by his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> Shields, he was handed over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> and executed in 316 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eumenes-of-cardia.jpg" alt="Eumenes of Cardia" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coins_of_Philip_III_Arrhidaeus._323-317_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
